Why did show() reach OPBill::total()?
Although show() is non-virtual, it calls the virtual function total(). The virtual function is resolved based on the actual object type at runtime, so OPBill::total() is called.
If virtual is removed from ~Bill
The destructor of the derived class will not be properly called when deleting through a Bill*. This causes undefined behaviour and may result in resource leaks. AddressSanitizer may report errors depending on the program and compiler.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If total() were not virtual, the program could use dynamic_cast to identify each bill type and call the corresponding function.

Example pseudocode:
IF bill is OutPatientBill
    CALL OutPatientBill total()
ELSE IF bill is InPatientBill
    CALL InPatientBill total()
END IF


Feature	Virtual Functions	dynamic_cast Approach
Time per bill	O(1)	Up to O(k) casts
Total time for n bills	O(n)	O(nk)
Space	Vtable pointer per object	RTTI information and casting overhead
Adding new bill type	Add new derived class	Modify type-checking code

Conclusion: Runtime polymorphism is better because it allows the correct function to be called automatically and makes the code easier to extend and maintain.
